<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dissolution of the Soviet Union</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Collapse of the Soviet Union, Breakup of the Soviet Union</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> was the breakup of the USSR into fifteen independent states in 1991. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a>'s reforms had loosened central control, and after a failed coup by hardliners in August 1991 the republics declared independence one after another. On 8 December the leaders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> agreed to replace the union with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-independent-states">Commonwealth of Independent States</a>; Gorbachev resigned on 25 December, and the Soviet Union formally ceased to exist the next day.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union.jpg" alt="Dissolution of the Soviet Union" width="330" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yuriy Kuydin / Юрий Куйдин · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIAN_archive_140800_Signing_of_Protocol_on_Establishing_Commonwealth_of_Independent_States.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a> was elected General Secretary on 11 March 1985, just over four hours after the death of Konstantin Chernenko. He meant to revive a stagnating economy and came to see that this required reforming political and social structures. His glasnost allowed nationalist movements and ethnic disputes to be expressed and to grow into dominant political forces. Commentators also point to chronic economic stagnation and the burden of the arms race with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>In 1989 limited competitive elections were held for a new Congress of People's Deputies, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1989">revolutions of 1989</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> countries increased pressure for republican autonomy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> was the first republic to declare state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, on 16 November 1988, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> the first to declare independence, in March 1990. In 1990 all fifteen republics held competitive elections, and the Communist Party lost them in five.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 17 March 1991 a Union-wide referendum was held in which 77.85 per cent of voters endorsed keeping a reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>; the Baltic republics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a> boycotted it. In August 1991 communist hardliners tried to overthrow Gorbachev. The coup failed, but the turmoil weakened the central government, and many republics declared independence in the following days and months.</p><p>On 8 December the leaders of the Russian, Byelorussian and Ukrainian republics signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belovezha-accords">Belovezha Accords</a>, declared that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> no longer existed and set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-independent-states">Commonwealth of Independent States</a>. Kazakhstan was the last republic to declare independence, on 16 December. Gorbachev resigned on 25 December and passed his powers, including control of the nuclear launch codes, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-yeltsin">Boris Yeltsin</a>, and the Soviet flag was lowered from the Kremlin. On 26 December the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-of-the-republics">Soviet of the Republics</a> formally dissolved the Soviet Union.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The dissolution marked the culmination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1989">revolutions of 1989</a> and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. Russia became the recognised successor state and continued the Soviet seat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. Several successor states kept close links with Russia through the Commonwealth, while the Baltic states moved towards the West. The end of Soviet aid brought crisis to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, and disputes over territory and minorities led to conflicts in some successor states.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dissolution of the Soviet Union” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With the Belovezha Accords and the <b>dissolution of the Soviet Union</b> on 26 December 1991 the Armenian SSR ceased to exist; its constitution of 1978 remained in force until July 1995.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After victory in 1945 the United States became one of two superpowers, and with the <b>dissolution of the Soviet Union</b> in 1991 it remained the only one.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Jadids have now been rehabilitated as “Uzbek National Heroes” in Uzbekistan, where they were recognised and honoured after the <b>dissolution of the Soviet Union</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/jadidism">Jadidism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It also set off a chain reaction across the Soviet Union’s satellite states, the mostly bloodless Revolutions of 1989, in Polish the Autumn of Nations, which overthrew each Moscow-imposed regime and led in the end to the <b>dissolution of the Soviet Union</b> in the early 1990s.</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>The end of the Cold War and the <b>dissolution of the Soviet Union</b> then made capitalism a truly global system, and the neoliberal global economy would have been impossible without the fall of communism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>That evening the Soviet flag was lowered from the Kremlin and replaced by the Russian tricolour, and the next day the <b>Soviet Union formally ceased to exist</b>.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li></ul></section>
